from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "4. The Lord’s Healings and Matthew 8:17"),
("p", "Claim: the Gospels place the Lord’s healings inside His ministry to Israel, and Matthew names one evening of healing as the fulfilment of a sentence in Isaiah. The healings come with the preaching of the kingdom, they answer John’s question about who the Lord is, and they are done to Israelites in the land. The chapter reads the Gospel passages in that order and sets Matthew 8:17 beside its source without joining it to any other passage."),
("sub", "4.1 Healing with the preaching of the kingdom"),
("p", "Matthew describes the work twice in the same words. “And Jesus went about all Galilee, teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing all manner of sickness and all manner of disease among the people” (Matthew 4:23). “And Jesus went about all the cities and villages, teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every sickness and every disease among the people” (Matthew 9:35). The two verbs stand together: preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing. The place is the synagogues, the cities and villages of Galilee, and the hearers are “the people,” the people of Israel."),
("p", "Luke records the Lord reading the prophet at Nazareth. “The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he hath anointed me to preach the gospel to the poor; he hath sent me to heal the brokenhearted, to preach deliverance to the captives, and recovering of sight to the blind, to set at liberty them that are bruised, To preach the acceptable year of the Lord” (Luke 4:18, 19). The healing is part of the work the Lord says He has been sent to do, and He says it in the synagogue of His own town."),
("sub", "4.2 John’s question"),
("p", "When John the Baptist was in prison he sent two disciples to ask the Lord, “Art thou he that should come, or do we look for another?” (Matthew 11:3). The Lord answered with what could be seen and heard: “Go and shew John again those things which ye do hear and see: The blind receive their sight, and the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, and the deaf hear, the dead are raised up, and the poor have the gospel preached to them” (Matthew 11:4, 5). The healings are given as the answer to a question about who He is. They are evidence addressed to Israel, asked by Israel’s prophet. The Lord adds, “And blessed is he, whosoever shall not be offended in me” (Matthew 11:6)."),
("sub", "4.3 Sent to Israel"),
("p", "The Lord states the bounds of His own sending in plain words. “But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (Matthew 15:24). The healings in Galilee are done within those bounds, and the Gospels record the crowds’ response in Israel’s terms: “and they glorified the God of Israel” (Matthew 15:31). The centurion at Capernaum is the one exception that the Gospel marks as an exception. The Lord said of him, “Verily I say unto you, I have not found so great faith, no, not in Israel” (Matthew 8:10), and added that “many shall come from the east and west, and shall sit down with Abraham, and Isaac, and Jacob, in the kingdom of heaven” (Matthew 8:11). The centurion’s servant was healed at a word spoken from a distance (Matthew 8:13), and the setting is still the kingdom that is preached to Israel."),
("sub", "4.4 Matthew 8:14 to 17"),
("p", "The healing that Matthew ties to Isaiah comes at Capernaum. “And when Jesus was come into Peter’s house, he saw his wife’s mother laid, and sick of a fever. And he touched her hand, and the fever left her: and she arose, and ministered unto them. When the even was come, they brought unto him many that were possessed with devils: and he cast out the spirits with his word, and healed all that were sick: That it might be fulfilled which was spoken by Esaias the prophet, saying, Himself took our infirmities, and bare our sicknesses” (Matthew 8:14–17). The sentence names an evening, a house, a crowd brought to the door and a result: the sick were healed and the spirits cast out. It says that this was done “that it might be fulfilled.” The fulfilment is named at the place and the time of the healing, before the cross."),
("p", "Matthew’s wording of the quotation differs from the KJV’s wording of Isaiah 53:4, which reads, “Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried our sorrows.” The volume reports both as the KJV prints them and builds nothing on the difference. What the Gospel states is that the Lord’s healing on that evening is what Isaiah’s sentence spoke of. Matthew does not say that the sentence speaks of every later sick person, and he does not say that it speaks of the cross."),
("sub", "4.5 Isaiah 53:4, 5 in its own chapter"),
("p", "Isaiah’s chapter opens with a question that is put by a people: “Who hath believed our report? and to whom is the arm of the LORD revealed?” (Isaiah 53:1). The speakers of the confession are a “we,” and the confession is that they esteemed the servant “stricken, smitten of God, and afflicted” (Isaiah 53:4). The next verse says, “But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed” (Isaiah 53:5). The word “healed” stands in the same sentence as “transgressions,” “iniquities” and “peace,” and the chapter ends on the same ground: “for he shall bear their iniquities” and “he bare the sin of many, and made intercession for the transgressors” (Isaiah 53:11, 12)."),
("p", "The volume reads the chapter as Israel’s national confession about the servant and as the prophecy of the atonement that answers it. Study 86 reads the servant passages of Isaiah in order and is the place for the question who the servant is. This volume asks only what the chapter says is healed. The text says that the stripes heal, and in the verse and its neighbours the thing healed is the transgressions and iniquities of the “we” who confess. Isaiah 53:4 and Matthew 8:17 meet at the Lord’s ministry, and Isaiah 53:5 meets 1 Peter 2:24 in chapter 11. The volume does not join the three."),
("sub", "4.6 The other Gospel uses of healing"),
("p", "John gives the purpose of the signs he records. “And many other signs truly did Jesus in the presence of his disciples, which are not written in this book: But these are written, that ye might believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God; and that believing ye might have life through his name” (John 20:30, 31). John’s healings are signs written for a purpose he states. John also quotes Isaiah on those who did not believe despite the signs, including the sentence that they should not “be converted, and I should heal them” (John 12:40). The word “heal” in that sentence is used of the nation’s turning. Volume 1 reads John’s signs and John 20:30, 31 in chapter 5, and this volume cites that reading."),
("p", "Matthew gives a further use of the prophets for the healings. “And great multitudes followed him, and he healed them all; And charged them that they should not make him known: That it might be fulfilled which was spoken by Esaias the prophet” (Matthew 12:15–17). The passage quoted is the first servant song of Isaiah 42. Healing appears again in the setting of fulfilment, and again before the cross."),
("tbl", "The Lord’s healings in Matthew.", [
    ("Matthew 4:23", T("Matt 4:23"), "Teaching, preaching the kingdom and healing, in the synagogues of Galilee."),
    ("Matthew 11:4, 5", T("Matt 11:4, 5"), "The healings are the answer to John’s question."),
    ("Matthew 15:24", T("Matt 15:24"), "The Lord states the bound of His sending."),
    ("Matthew 8:17", T("Matt 8:17"), "The evening’s healings are named as the fulfilment of Isaiah’s sentence."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The Lord’s healings are done in Israel, with the preaching of the kingdom, and by His own account within the bounds of His sending.",
    "John the Baptist’s question is answered with the healings as evidence of who the Lord is.",
    "Matthew 8:17 names the healings at Capernaum as the fulfilment of Isaiah’s sentence, before the cross.",
    "In Isaiah 53 the stripes heal, and the thing named as healed is the transgression and iniquity of the confessing people.",
    "Isaiah 53:4, 5, Matthew 8:17 and 1 Peter 2:24 are kept in their own settings.",
  ]),
]
